The '''Toyozawa Dainichi Department Store fire''' was a catastrophic structural fire that occurred in the Tennoji Ward of [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]], on October 21, 1976. The disaster resulted in 164 fatalities and remains the deadliest building fire in Sanese history. The tragedy is cited as the primary catalyst for the '''1978 Building Fire Safety Act''', which fundamentally restructured the nation's fire codes and mandated the installation of automated suppression systems in multi-use commercial structures.

== Venue ==
[[File:Dainichi1969.png|250px|thumb|float|left|The Toyozawa Dainichi Department Store, in 1969.]]
The Dainichi Building was a prominent "conglomerate structure" (''zasshū biru'') located near the Toyozawa Central hub. At the time of the fire, the building's layout was complex: the basement housed a grocery market and several small eateries; the first through fourth floors were occupied by the Dainichi Department Store; the fifth and sixth floors contained a bowling alley and a children's arcade; and the seventh floor was home to the "Midnight Sun" (''Mayonaka no Taiyō''), a high-occupancy nightclub and cabaret.

The building had originally been constructed in the early 1950s as a theater and lacked modern safety features. Due to the decentralized management of the various tenants, there was no unified fire safety plan. Many stairwells were utilized by different shops for storage, and the seventh-floor cabaret had only one accessible public exit, as the emergency doors were frequently locked to prevent non-paying patrons from entering.

== Fire ==
On the evening of October 21, 1976, at approximately 20:45, a fire broke out on the third floor in a storage area containing synthetic fabrics and seasonal decorations. Early investigations suggested the cause was either a smouldering cigarette discarded by a stock clerk or an electrical short circuit in an outdated wiring harness.

Because the third and fourth floors were undergoing minor renovations, many of the fire-shutters had been manually disabled to allow for the passage of construction equipment. The fire spread with extreme rapidity through the open elevator shafts and stairwells. The highly flammable materials in the department store produced thick, black toxic smoke that quickly bypassed the lower floors and funneled directly into the seventh-floor nightclub.

The "Midnight Sun" was at maximum capacity with over 250 patrons when the smoke arrived. mass panic ensued as the elevators ceased to function and the main stairwell became a chimney for heat and gas. Cabaret staff, familiar with a service ladder at the rear of the kitchen, evacuated themselves without alerting the customers.

Eyewitnesses reported seeing dozens of people breaking through the reinforced windows and attempting to climb down the building's facade. Responding firefighters from the Toyozawa Metropolitan Fire Bureau arrived at 21:02, but their efforts were hampered by the building's narrow access roads and the height of the blaze.

== Victims ==
Of the 164 deceased, the vast majority were discovered on the seventh floor. Post-mortem examinations revealed that 85% of the victims died of carbon monoxide poisoning before the flames ever reached the top floor. Responders described a haunting scene in the cabaret, where many victims were found still seated at their tables, having been overcome by toxic fumes almost instantly. Approximately 32 people died from injuries sustained while jumping from the upper floors, and 12 were killed in the crush near the locked emergency exits.

== Legal and Aftermath ==
The public outcry following the disaster led to the "Dainichi Trials," where building administrators and cabaret owners were prosecuted for gross occupational negligence. In 1980, four executives were sentenced to prison terms of five to seven years.

The most lasting legacy of the tragedy was the '''Building Fire Safety Act of 1978'''. This legislation mandated:
* The retroactive installation of automatic sprinkler systems in all commercial buildings over five stories.
* The legal requirement for a "Unified Safety Manager" in multi-tenant buildings.
* Strict "Clearance Mandates" for all stairwells and emergency exits, with heavy fines for storage violations.
* The establishment of the OSDF-Standardized emergency escape chute in high-density urban centers.

The ruins of the Dainichi Department Store were demolished in 1979. The site now serves as the '''Toyozawa Peace and Safety Plaza''', featuring a monument to the victims and a modern training facility for the metropolitan fire services.

